WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:24.840
<v Casey>Hey, welcome back. Today we've got Madison Square Garden's facial recognition network stretching across three major venues, a bill in Albany trying to ban it outright, and Google quietly letting Gemini users switch off AI watermarks on generated music and video. The question we're chasing: who actually gets to verify what's real, and who's stuck taking a company's word for it? Coming right up.

00:00:40.815 --> 00:01:02.251
<v Casey>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities — I'm Casey. It's Monday, August 24th, 2026. Coming to you from the Cariboo, on lands that have been cared for by the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh peoples since time immemorial. Today's theme is Arts, Culture &amp; Digital Storytelling.

00:01:03.113 --> 00:01:15.310
<v Riley>And I'm Riley. We're talking about who gets to make music and put on a show out here, what the tools cost in dollars, and what they cost in something harder to price.

00:01:15.569 --> 00:01:38.853
<v Casey>Quick weather check before we get into it. Twelve degrees at Horsefly Lake this morning, overcast, high of twenty-four expected, tomorrow looking cloudy again. Around the region — ten in 100 Mile House with mainly clear skies, thirteen in Williams Lake under cloud, thirteen in Quesnel and clear, and out on the Chilcotin plateau, Tatla Lake's sitting at eleven with clear skies too.

00:01:39.112 --> 00:01:45.025
<v Riley>Good day to be outside before the smoke rolls back in. Let's get into what's happening.

00:01:46.426 --> 00:01:53.514
<v Casey>Four stories close to home today, a real cluster on the arts and tech side, and a longer tail than usual to close things out.

00:01:54.721 --> 00:02:16.809
<v Riley>Starting with something worth sitting with. The Williams Lake Tribune covered Saturday's Pride Parade, and the headline says it plainly — love speaks louder than online hate. Organizers reported harassment showing up in comments and direct messages in the weeks leading into the event, some of it ugly enough that people asked whether the parade should even go ahead this year.

00:02:16.709 --> 00:02:17.901
<v Casey>And it did.

00:02:19.234 --> 00:02:37.130
<v Riley>It went ahead, well attended, with families and kids along the route according to the paper's photos. The piece makes the point that the loudest voice online isn't always the room. A parade in a town of ten thousand doesn't need a hundred thousand likes to matter. It needs the people who showed up on the street.

00:02:38.330 --> 00:03:01.722
<v Casey>Less good news out of 100 Mile House. Interior Health has announced the emergency department at 100 Mile District General Hospital will close overnight starting Friday, August 28th at seven o'clock. That's a temporary service interruption, in Interior Health's language, but for South Cariboo residents it means anyone with an after-dark emergency is driving to Williams Lake instead.

00:03:02.995 --> 00:03:10.179
<v Riley>That's the better part of an hour each way for people in town, longer from the outlying areas around Lac La Hache or Forest Grove.

00:03:11.306 --> 00:03:22.394
<v Casey>No end date given yet for when overnight service resumes. It's the kind of gap that gets absorbed by families who don't have another option, and it barely registers outside the region.

00:03:23.594 --> 00:03:48.154
<v Riley>Fire is still shaping how people move around this province. Highway 1 between Yale and Boston Bar remains closed because of the Ainslie Creek fire, and the Tribune's update gives no timeline for reopening. That highway is the main artery connecting the Interior to the coast — anyone routing freight or making a supply run toward the Lower Mainland right now is looking at a long detour through the Coquihalla, or waiting it out.

00:03:49.612 --> 00:04:07.772
<v Casey>And further east, the City of Revelstoke has issued an evacuation order for five rural properties near Beaton, tied to the Johnson Creek wildfire. Four people and their pets were airlifted out safely over the weekend after the fire cut off ground access, and the order stays in effect while crews work the fire's edge.

00:04:09.074 --> 00:04:14.562
<v Riley>Two highway-adjacent fires, two disrupted communities, and it's not even September yet.

00:04:15.462 --> 00:04:38.006
<v Casey>On the culture side — the Southern Alberta Art Gallery in Lethbridge has broken ground on a full revitalization, led by the architecture firm DIALOG. The project reworks the existing interior spaces and adds new indoor and outdoor amenities, the kind of capital renewal that takes years of planning and a professional design team most rural galleries never get near.

00:04:39.138 --> 00:04:48.466
<v Riley>The gap between a gallery with an architecture firm on retainer and a hall running on volunteer labour is the whole story of arts infrastructure funding in one sentence.

00:04:49.679 --> 00:05:11.087
<v Casey>Business Insider has a less flattering technology story. Instagram promised, months ago, to remove harassing videos filmed with Meta's AI-enabled smart glasses — people using the built-in camera to record and mock strangers in public without consent. The reporter went back through the platform recently and found dozens of those videos are still live.

00:05:10.987 --> 00:05:18.483
<v Riley>Enforcement promises are cheap. Moderation at scale is the actual product, and they keep underdelivering on it.

00:05:19.868 --> 00:05:39.604
<v Casey>The Western Producer's piece nominally covers the U.S. edible bean sector — American growers cutting seeded acres after prices collapsed — but what actually came through in the article body was mostly a plot summary of a horror film, which tells you something about how thin some wire content gets by the end of a slow news week. We'll leave that one there.

00:05:40.812 --> 00:05:56.420
<v Riley>Forbes has a sturdier story though. Mass timber construction is drawing serious capital from big tech firms and major developers, driven in part by data centre builders looking for a lower-carbon alternative to steel and concrete for large structures.

00:05:57.759 --> 00:06:12.551
<v Casey>Mass timber is a BC industry story too, whether the coverage says so or not — a meaningful share of the engineered wood going into those buildings starts as Interior spruce and pine that gets milled and glue-laminated before it ever leaves the province.

00:06:13.451 --> 00:06:32.307
<v Riley>A handful of quick ones to round things out. Physicists working with data from the RHIC particle collider at Brookhaven National Laboratory say they may have found a new gluon structure inside protons — something about how the particle's baryon number gets distributed that could revise textbook models of what a proton is actually made of.

00:06:33.747 --> 00:06:54.531
<v Casey>WIRED's got a heads-up for planners — two major solar eclipses are coming in 2027 and 2028. The 2027 one is being called the eclipse of the century for its unusually long duration, and 2028 brings an annular "ring of fire" event visible from a different swath of the continent.

00:06:55.846 --> 00:07:11.942
<v Casey>The Verge is flagging a change to Amazon's terms of service. Customers got an email update saying future disputes with the company will go through arbitration instead of the courts, a move consumer advocates say is designed to head off class-action lawsuits before they can start.

00:07:13.284 --> 00:07:27.916
<v Riley>And a health update out of Victoria — BC's finance minister, Brenda Bailey, says she's been diagnosed with early-stage, treatable cancer. She'll be receiving treatment over the summer and expects to be back at work in time for the fall legislative session.

00:07:29.033 --> 00:07:45.081
<v Casey>Our World in Data published a longer-view piece worth flagging — several countries have already passed peak population, and researchers are mapping out when the rest of the world is expected to follow as fertility rates keep declining across most regions.

00:07:46.308 --> 00:08:02.908
<v Riley>And in London, the Underground has started trialing live facial recognition cameras in Tube stations, a rollout from the British Transport Police that began earlier this week. Privacy groups are pushing back hard, arguing the system treats every rider like a suspect before anything's happened.

00:08:03.408 --> 00:08:09.456
<v Casey>Hold that thought. Facial recognition at public venues is very much on the agenda for the second half of the show.

00:08:10.756 --> 00:08:29.204
<v Riley>Before we get to the spotlight, one more story that deserves the full telling. The Tyee published a piece on what happens after a wildfire evacuation order lifts and people come home to freezers full of rotted meat. BC used to fund the collection and disposal of those ruined appliances after long evacuations. That funding has stopped.

00:08:30.409 --> 00:08:48.041
<v Casey>The ministry's response to the Tyee's questions was that residents should contact their insurers, that the province reimburses eligible costs to local governments, and that staff were "connecting directly" with the Village of Clinton and the Thompson-Nicola Regional District to understand their needs.

00:08:49.157 --> 00:09:04.437
<v Riley>None of which explains why the program stopped in the first place. Home insurance doesn't cover a community-wide public health problem — a street lined with rotting freezers is a shared hazard, not an individual claim, and somebody still has to haul them away.

00:09:05.642 --> 00:09:17.626
<v Casey>It's the kind of gap that only surfaces once the news cameras and the evacuation order are gone, and this region has been through enough of these cycles now that it shouldn't be a surprise every single time it happens again.

00:09:19.026 --> 00:09:42.674
<v Riley>With school starting back up, it's a good moment to mention Cariboo Chilcotin Partners for Literacy. They run free adult literacy, English language, and digital skills programs across the region, whether you're brushing up on reading, learning English as a new language, or trying to figure out email and online banking for the first time. No cost, no judgment, just people helping people. Find them at caribooliteracy dot com.

00:09:43.274 --> 00:09:52.674
<v Casey>Digital skills programs matter more every year this economy pushes basic transactions online — good to know there's somewhere to go that isn't a paywall.

00:09:53.251 --> 00:10:00.195
<v Riley>Which is actually a decent bridge into today's Deep Dive — trust, tools, and who gets to decide what's real.

00:10:01.595 --> 00:10:23.611
<v Casey>Start with Madison Square Garden. Lawmakers, musicians, and privacy advocates rallied outside the arena this month because MSG has been running facial recognition on every single person who walks through the door, and reporting alleges the venue has used it to identify and eject people the ownership doesn't like. Lawyers involved in litigation against the company. Journalists. Critics.

00:10:23.935 --> 00:10:27.639
<v Riley>Security tech doing security things, until the day it doesn't.

00:10:28.283 --> 00:10:52.843
<v Casey>That's the whole arc, in two sentences. It gets sold to the public as crowd safety — nobody's going to argue against catching a known threat before they get near the stage. Then it becomes a blacklist tool nobody voted on, running on a system nobody outside the company is allowed to inspect. Reporting says the network doesn't even stop at the Garden. It reaches Radio City and the Beacon Theatre too.

00:10:53.444 --> 00:11:04.476
<v Riley>So one company's face-scanning network covers three of the most-watched entertainment venues in the country, and none of the ten thousand people walking through those doors on a given night get a vote on it.

00:11:05.143 --> 00:11:32.119
<v Casey>Right. And the question underneath all of that isn't really about facial recognition as a technology. It's about who's allowed to open that system up and check what it's actually doing. Not the ticket holder. Not the musician playing the room. Apparently not even the New York City Council, which is why Albany lawmakers are pushing a bill — reporters have taken to calling it "Madison Spy Garden" — to ban the tech in public venues statewide rather than just asking MSG to explain itself.

00:11:32.665 --> 00:11:48.577
<v Riley>There's a group called Fight for the Future organizing musicians against this setup — Tom Morello, Kathleen Hanna, artists whose own fans fill those seats, standing outside the building pushing back on the surveillance infrastructure wrapped around their own concerts.

00:11:49.165 --> 00:12:09.005
<v Casey>Which is a strange loop. The artist draws the crowd. The venue scans the crowd. And the artist has zero say in what happens to that scan afterward. It's not even a new problem — California's biometric privacy law traces partly back to facial recognition scanning at a Taylor Swift concert at the Rose Bowl back in 2019.

00:12:09.505 --> 00:12:25.633
<v Riley>The angle that matters for us: no venue in the Cariboo is installing facial recognition. Nobody here has the crowd size or the budget to justify it. In one sense, we're locked out of that entire surveillance economy without even trying.

00:12:25.911 --> 00:12:29.671
<v Casey>(wry) You're framing "locked out" like it's a consolation prize.

00:12:30.288 --> 00:12:49.984
<v Riley>I'm framing it as leverage nobody's using yet. A community hall in Horsefly doesn't need to solve MSG's problem. It needs its sound board fixed and its booking calendar working. Different scale, different tools, and none of it requires asking a security contractor's permission to pop a panel open.

00:12:50.536 --> 00:13:17.416
<v Casey>Except the panel you're describing isn't the one that actually matters here. The Gemini watermark story is a lot closer to home than anything happening at MSG. Google just let users toggle off the visible watermark on AI-generated music, images, and video made through the Gemini app. The invisible tag — SynthID — is still baked in underneath, but nobody sees it unless they go and actively ask Gemini to check.

00:13:17.745 --> 00:13:20.393
<v Riley>Which almost nobody will bother doing.

00:13:21.023 --> 00:13:43.847
<v Casey>Right. So picture a small-town theatre production using an AI-generated backing score because there's no budget for a composer this season. That's a defensible cost-saving choice on its own. But there's no visible marker left telling the audience, or a reviewer, or a grant jury evaluating "original local work," that any of it was AI-made.

00:13:44.376 --> 00:14:05.056
<v Riley>Ars Technica's own testing found the invisible tag holds up technically — it's hard to strip out. The engineering isn't the failure point. The failure point is that verifying it takes effort, and almost nobody spends that effort. Regulators have already been told watermarking text at scale isn't practically feasible either.

00:14:05.732 --> 00:14:18.308
<v Casey>So the accountability exists on paper and functions like it doesn't exist at all. That's arguably worse than having no watermark, because it lets everyone point at the feature list and call the problem solved.

00:14:18.808 --> 00:14:43.440
<v Riley>Push on that a bit further, because there's a real question buried in it about who's allowed to fix this. SynthID is proprietary. A rural arts council has no way to audit it themselves even if they wanted to. There's no local technician, no open standard, nobody in Quesnel who can independently verify whether a recording's provenance tag is even intact. Everyone downstream just has to take Google's word for it.

00:14:44.106 --> 00:14:56.298
<v Casey>Same shape as the MSG problem, honestly. A system built by one company, unverifiable by anyone who actually has to live with its consequences, and everyone else is stuck trusting the label on the box.

00:14:56.890 --> 00:15:13.194
<v Riley>There's a real difference in the stakes, though. Nobody gets hurt if a community theatre uses an AI backing track without flagging it loudly. Somebody does get hurt if a facial recognition system flags the wrong person at the door of an arena and a security guard acts on it.

00:15:13.739 --> 00:15:25.451
<v Casey>The harm's smaller, granted. But the trust question underneath is identical. Who gets to call something authentic, and who gets to challenge that call when they think it's wrong?

00:15:25.950 --> 00:15:45.502
<v Riley>Grant juries here are already wrestling with a version of that, actually. Arts funding runs on volunteer panels assessing "originality" and "local voice," categories built around an assumption that a human made the thing being judged. Nobody's built a review process yet for work that's AI-assisted but human-directed and locally performed.

00:15:46.162 --> 00:16:03.482
<v Casey>And nobody's going to build one fast enough to matter. Small arts councils run on unpaid boards reading applications on weekday evenings. Asking them to also become AI-provenance auditors is asking volunteers to do a job Google itself won't reliably do.

00:16:04.065 --> 00:16:24.153
<v Riley>That's a real problem, but the fix doesn't have to be technical. A one-line disclosure on the funding application — did AI tools contribute to this work, and how — self-reported, not independently verified. It's cheap, and it at least puts the question on the record instead of pretending it doesn't exist.

00:16:24.752 --> 00:16:37.440
<v Casey>Self-reported disclosure only works if the applicant has a reason to be honest about it. If the AI-assisted score gets funded either way, what's the incentive to check that box truthfully?

00:16:37.760 --> 00:16:56.240
<v Riley>The incentive isn't about the funding decision. It's about the audience knowing what they're watching. A theatre society that's upfront about using AI for part of a soundtrack gets to have that conversation with its own community directly, on its own terms, instead of it surfacing later as a scandal after somebody figures it out.

00:16:56.806 --> 00:17:02.622
<v Casey>It's a small mechanism against a large problem, and it only works if people actually use it.

00:17:03.522 --> 00:17:18.210
<v Riley>There's a stranger data point sitting in this week's stack, too. Engadget flagged a study showing a fifteen percent jump in traffic deaths on days a major album drops — the theory being drivers get distracted streaming the new release while they're behind the wheel.

00:17:18.731 --> 00:17:35.627
<v Casey>That's a stretch to tie into today's thread directly, but it says something about how deeply music streaming has embedded itself into daily behaviour, distraction risk included. Nobody designing an album's release calendar was thinking about drivers on the highway when they picked the drop date.

00:17:36.282 --> 00:17:45.722
<v Riley>Nobody asked drivers either. It's the same pattern running through the whole hour — systems get built by people who aren't the ones absorbing the consequences downstream.

00:17:46.622 --> 00:18:04.326
<v Casey>Bring this back to a stage somebody in the Cariboo could actually book. A touring act plays a mid-size room in Kamloops or Kelowna with a professional sound and lighting rig behind them, then plays a community hall out here running on a board that's twenty years old, kept alive by one volunteer who happens to know how to patch it.

00:18:05.006 --> 00:18:22.502
<v Riley>And when that board eventually dies, who's allowed to fix it? If it's proprietary digital gear, the same question that applies to MSG's surveillance panel applies to a small hall's mixing console — manufacturer-only support, a certified technician, a service call from Kamloops that can cost more than the gig itself paid out.

00:18:23.167 --> 00:18:42.847
<v Casey>Which is the actual case for keeping analog gear alive in these rooms longer than the industry wants anyone to. A forty-year-old mixing board with knobs and a schematic anyone can learn to solder is repairable by more people, more cheaply, than a networked digital board that phones home to a manufacturer for firmware updates it may never get again.

00:18:43.161 --> 00:18:46.569
<v Riley>(chuckles) So the highest tech in the room stays a soldering iron.

00:18:46.669 --> 00:19:07.877
<v Riley>That's the real version of "who's allowed to fix it" for music venues out here. It isn't facial recognition, and it isn't AI watermarking. It's whether the gear a hall owns can be kept alive by the community that owns it, or whether every repair has to route through a company two provinces away that may not even support the model in five years.

00:19:08.377 --> 00:19:35.009
<v Casey>Williams Lake has venues running exactly that mix right now — some newer digital boards for the bigger shows, some old analog holdouts kept going because one person locally actually understands the guts of them. Cultural events run through local First Nations organizations tend to lean on simpler, portable PA setups for a related reason. Less to break, and what does break can get fixed on-site instead of shipped out.

00:19:35.366 --> 00:19:39.718
<v Riley>Simplicity as a resilience strategy, not just a budget constraint.

00:19:40.293 --> 00:19:49.621
<v Casey>Both, honestly. The effect on the ground is the same either way. Capacity stays local instead of leaking out to a service contract somewhere else.

00:19:50.121 --> 00:20:03.713
<v Riley>So where does that leave the AI watermark thread? Because that one doesn't have an obvious analog-gear equivalent. There's no simpler tool that just sidesteps the provenance problem the way an old mixing board sidesteps the firmware problem.

00:20:04.332 --> 00:20:24.404
<v Casey>There might be a partial one. A recording made on a phone, in a real room, with a real audience in it — the context is the provenance. It's not verified by an invisible digital tag, it's verified by twenty people who were physically there and posted their own footage of the same set from their own seats.

00:20:24.962 --> 00:20:37.410
<v Riley>That works for a hundred-seat hall where everyone knows everyone. It doesn't scale to an artist trying to build an audience outside the region, which is half of what "reaching listeners beyond geography" is actually supposed to mean for this theme.

00:20:38.067 --> 00:20:44.155
<v Casey>No argument there. It's a small-room answer to a problem that gets bigger the further the work travels.

00:20:45.054 --> 00:20:53.414
<v Riley>What's an actual, concrete thing a hall could try this fall, instead of waiting on Google or a provincial arts body to sort any of this out?

00:20:53.701 --> 00:21:06.589
<v Casey>Post a plain disclosure with every locally produced recording that goes online. Who played, what instrumentation, whether any part of it was AI-generated. One line in the video description, no committee, no application form.

00:21:07.214 --> 00:21:16.414
<v Riley>It doesn't solve provenance at any real scale. It puts the choice out in the open for the one room that actually made the thing, which is more than exists right now.

00:21:17.028 --> 00:21:26.556
<v Casey>It's a maintenance cost of its own, though. Someone has to remember to write that line every time, forever, with nothing stopping them from just skipping it when it's inconvenient.

00:21:27.162 --> 00:21:36.346
<v Riley>Every disclosure standard carries that same weakness. The alternative on the table right now is no disclosure standard at all, which is where things sit today.

00:21:36.846 --> 00:21:52.958
<v Casey>Fine. It's a small, doable step. It still doesn't touch the bigger problem sitting at the MSG end of this — a company deciding who's allowed in a building based on a face scan nobody consented to, with a city council scrambling to catch up after the fact.

00:21:53.485 --> 00:22:09.045
<v Riley>No, and it's not supposed to. That fight belongs to city councils and provincial legislators, not to a volunteer board running a hall in Horsefly. Ours is a smaller problem, and the upside is we can actually act on it without asking anyone outside the region for permission.

00:22:09.648 --> 00:22:27.120
<v Casey>That's the version of this I can get behind — not because the surveillance question at MSG doesn't matter, it does, but because it isn't ours to fix from here, and pretending otherwise just wastes the hour a volunteer board actually has. Though I'd bet the Albany fight drags on longer than any of us expect.

00:22:28.020 --> 00:22:40.564
<v Riley>If you run a venue or a small production and want to compare notes on gear, disclosure lines, or just what's breaking and who locally can still fix it, write in — feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a.

00:22:41.201 --> 00:22:51.361
<v Casey>And if you're the one person left in this region who still knows how to solder a thirty-year-old mixing board, we'd love to hear from you before that knowledge walks out the door.

00:22:51.666 --> 00:22:53.386
<v Riley>Have a great rest of your day.

00:22:53.286 --> 00:22:55.206
<v Casey>(deadpan) Go easy on the smoke out there.